            'Soil drainage: The most commonly overlooked site factor. Most conifers absolutely require well-drained soil — planting arborvitae, spruce, or fir in a low-lying wet area almost guarantees failure. Exceptions: sweetbay magnolia, bald cypress, Atlantic white cedar, wax myrtle, and inkberry holly tolerate wet conditions. If in doubt, dig a 12-inch hole, fill with water, and check drainage: if water sits for more than an hour, drainage is poor.',
